{
  "meta": {
    "title": "Whale Fall Sulfide Stage: Osedax's Bacterial Partners",
    "date": "2026-08-15",
    "lesson": "A dead whale on the abyssal floor becomes a decades-long sulfide oasis. As anaerobic microbes strip lipids from bone, they release sulfide that fuels chemosynthetic sulfur-oxidizing bacteria living as symbionts and free mats on the carcass. This scenario models the sulfophilic stage: a lipid-rich bone substrate feeds heterotrophs while a chemolithotroph bank draws energy from a shallow sulfide plume seeping from the bone itself, not from any particle. Low light, near-freezing cold, and a slow, patient metabolism define the deep sea; the reward is a chemical plume worth staying in rather than food worth chasing.",
    "citation": "Smith & Baco 2003, Annu. Rev. Ecol. Syst.; Goffredi et al. 2005 on Osedax bone-eating worms",
    "difficulty": "hard",
    "realWorldBasis": "Whale-fall sulfophilic stage, bathyal Pacific, ~1000-4000m depth"
  },
